# DialCrypt: let the App connect to aegiscalld's abstract Unix socket
# @io.github.h4lovoid.dialcrypt.d. aegiscalld is started by service.sh and therefore runs in
# the root manager's domain: ksu under KernelSU (measured on the test phones,
# u:r:ksu:s0) or magisk under Magisk.
#
# This is the smallest rule SELinux can express for that:
#   - one source type (priv_app; the App is installed as a privileged app),
#   - one class (unix_stream_socket) and one permission (connectto),
#   - no sendto, no datagram sockets, no binder, no file or process access.
# SELinux cannot match an abstract socket by name, so the rule also covers
# other stream sockets a magisk-domain process listens on. The name-level
# restriction is enforced by the daemon itself: it accepts a peer only after
# checking SO_PEERCRED uid -> packages.list -> the package manager's signing
# record against the pinned certificate, and closes every other connection
# before reading any message.
#
# To be confirmed on device (target Pixel, Magisk version in use):
#   1. The App's domain is priv_app (if it is platform_app or untrusted_app,
#      change the source type; never widen it to "domain" or "appdomain").
#   2. Whether Magisk's built-in policy already allows this connection. If
#      it does, delete this rule and ship an empty file.
#   3. Whether a dedicated domain for aegiscalld (type + transition) is
#      workable; that would remove the magisk domain from this rule.
#   4. KernelSU: that the ksu rule below loads (the ksu type exists only on
#      KernelSU, the magisk type only on Magisk; check that a rule naming the
#      missing type is skipped and does not stop the rest of this file) and
#      that the App can connect with it. The ksu rule is listed first so that
#      it still applies if the loader stops at the first unknown type.
allow priv_app ksu unix_stream_socket connectto
allow priv_app magisk unix_stream_socket connectto
